WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:19.180
THE STATE OF IBM:
Once upon a time, IBM was a household name.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:23.110
They were the most well known computer manufacturer
in the world period.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:28.850
But, today, while people still recognize the
name, virtually no one buys computers from

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:30.000
IBM.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.970
In fact, you can’t buy an IBM computer today
even if you wanted given that they sold the

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.970
business to Lenovo nearly 20 years ago.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:43.260
This is quite surprising given that by all
accounts, IBM was very much a first mover

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:44.809
within the PC industry.

00:00:44.809 --> 00:00:51.610
They launched their first PC way back in 1981
and I mean the company’s name itself is

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:53.620
International Business Machine.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:01:00.129
Yet, by the late 1980s, IBM was rapidly losing
marketshare to newcomers like Dell and Compaq.

00:01:00.129 --> 00:01:04.940
Heck, even the operating system that they
funded had turned into Microsoft.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:10.520
This downtrend only got worse in the 1990s
as IBM would go onto post the worst quarter

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:14.690
in corporate history having lost $4.96 billion.

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:20.360
IBM’s market cap would halve in value while
the S&amp;P 500 nearly doubled.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:25.290
Fortunately for IBM, the dotcom bubble would
give them some room to breathe as investors

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.720
were lenient to say the least.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:32.591
But, as the dotcom bubble collapsed in the
early 2000s, focus shifted back to IBM’s

00:01:32.591 --> 00:01:34.940
underperformance within the PC market.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:39.860
And this eventually led to IBM offloading
their most recognizable business.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:44.350
Considering the situation, you would think
that IBM was headed towards bankruptcy or

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.630
at the very least a painful decline.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:50.260
But, the exact opposite would actually take
place.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:54.829
IBM’s revenue, earnings, and market cap
would all skyrocket.

00:01:54.829 --> 00:01:59.720
And IBM would even overtake Microsoft for
a couple of years until Microsoft had their

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:00.950
own resurgence.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:07.420
Today, IBM stands at a marketcap of a $133
billion which makes them the world’s 90th

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:08.500
largest company.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:12.580
Clearly, losing within the PC industry hasn’t
killed off IBM altogether.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:19.879
So, here’s the rise, fall, and resurrection
of one of the most iconic brands of all time.

00:02:19.879 --> 00:02:25.819
FOUNDING IBM:
Taking a look back, the roots of IBM actually

00:02:25.819 --> 00:02:31.620
date back to the 1800s to 4 different companies
which included the Bundy Manufacturing Company,

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:35.720
the Tabulating Machine Company, the International
Time Recording Company, and the Computing

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:37.430
Scale Company of America.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:41.410
By far the most important of these was the
Tabulating Machine Company.

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:45.870
This company focused on creating data processing
machines for punch cards.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:50.840
This proved to be relatively successful given
that it was a must have at manufacturing plants

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:51.840
and factories.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.230
But the problem was that revenue from this
business was sporadic and unreliable.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:03:01.610
I mean, how many times are prominent factories
going to need to buy punch card machines?

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:04.360
Maybe, once every few years if that.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:09.050
So, the founder of the company, Herman Hollerith,
would shift his focus to a larger project,

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:10.830
the 1900 US census.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:16.090
The census challenged his machines like never
before due to the raw volume and scale, but

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:21.590
Herman would successfully complete the project
which gave him some notoriety within the industry.

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:25.470
Despite this though, it didn’t take him
long to fall back to ground zero.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:31.819
You see, the US only conducts a census once
every 10 years, so revenue became sporadic

00:03:31.819 --> 00:03:34.040
and unreliable once again.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:38.620
While Herman very much wanted to turn this
around, he was getting into his 50s and his

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:40.489
health wasn’t quite holding up.

00:03:40.489 --> 00:03:45.930
So, he decided to sell the company to a man
named Charles Flint for $2.3 million.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:49.819
Flint would combine this with a few other
businesses and create a new company called

00:03:49.819 --> 00:03:51.319
CTR.

00:03:51.319 --> 00:03:56.080
And very quickly, it became obvious that Flint
was no rookie at business.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:58.990
He didn’t change anything technical about
the company.

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:03.510
Instead, he simply focused on expanding the
use cases and the sales force.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:08.090
He would hire an executive named Thomas J
Watson, and Watson would basically become

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:10.190
the father of IBM.

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:15.079
The first thing that Watson did was hire a
bunch of salesmen and teach them the intricacies

00:04:15.079 --> 00:04:16.709
of enterprise sales.

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:21.690
He instilled the values of company pride,
unbeatable customer service, and professionalism.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:25.940
Watson also expanded the scope of the company
from just selling punch card machines to providing

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:29.130
corporations with large scale tabulating solutions.

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:33.740
This turned out to be extremely successful
and Watson would end up doubling the company’s

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:36.620
revenue within just 4 years of taking over.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:43.580
Very soon, CTR would begin expanding internationally
to Europe, South America, Asia, and Australia.

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:47.330
Watson would also hone in on the companies
branding and public image.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:52.500
One of his first branding efforts was creating
a company slogan, and for this, he would just

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:56.040
reuse a slogan that he thought at his last
job: THINK.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.509
You might recognize this slogan today with
the Lenovo Thinkpad.

00:04:59.509 --> 00:05:04.500
Anyway, Watson now had a company slogan, but
there was still something that was really

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:05.620
erking him.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:07.800
Watson hated the name CTR.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:13.060
He felt that it was clunky and confining,
so in 1924, he would change the name of the

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:16.760
company to IBM or International Business Machine.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:21.510
It seems like this name change may have been
a bit unlucky though as the Great Depression

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:24.500
would role around not much later.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:30.949
INDUSTRY LEADER:
The great depression would hit IBM hard.

00:05:30.949 --> 00:05:36.670
Given that businesses were collapsing left
and right and given that IBM was a B2B business,

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:39.520
they had an extremely tough time keeping clients.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.900
Nonetheless, Watson didn’t panic.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:45.319
He was a strong believer in the idea that
if you had a great team that was building

00:05:45.319 --> 00:05:48.199
useful products, demand would follow.

00:05:48.199 --> 00:05:52.150
Watson would not only hire more employees
during the great depression, but he would

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:54.180
even increase their benefits.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:58.830
IBM was one of the first companies to provide
group life insurance, survivor benefits, and

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.130
paid vacations.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:04.289
But, despite Watson’s positive approach,
the effects of the great depression were very

00:06:04.289 --> 00:06:06.539
much evident throughout IBM.

00:06:06.539 --> 00:06:11.699
IBM was building up a massive inventory of
tabulating equipment with no market to sell

00:06:11.699 --> 00:06:12.699
to.

00:06:12.699 --> 00:06:18.380
But, as Watson was hoping, an opportunity
would eventually arise and it was massive.

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:23.770
In 1935, FDR would sign into effect the social
security act which required tabulating employment

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:26.360
records for 26 million people.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:31.580
IBM was the only company that bid on this
contract and by the end of the depression,

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:35.460
IBM would evolve from being a mid size company
to being an industry leader.

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:40.880
And their dominance would only grow with the
onset of WW2 as IBM’s tabulating equipment

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:43.330
would prove instrumental in the war.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:48.580
The allied forces widely used IBM’s equipment
for mobile record units, ballistics, accounting

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:51.430
and logistics, and of course punch cards.

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:56.570
To keep up with all this demand, IBM would
grow their manufacturing capacity exponentially,

00:06:56.570 --> 00:07:00.300
opening factories all across the North East
and California.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:04.130
Despite all this growth, Watson had a fear
in the back of his mind.

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:09.100
What would happen when the war came to an
end and government spending was cut substantially?

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:13.360
Watson began addressing this issue early on
to prepare as much as possible.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.430
He increased IBM’s international presence
and played a major role in the formation of

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:19.020
the World Trade Corporation.

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:23.490
But, it turned out that none of this preparation
was even required.

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:28.389
As soon as WW2 came to an end, the US would
get knees deep into the Cold War.

00:07:28.389 --> 00:07:31.849
The government no longer needed tabulating
equipment per se though.

00:07:31.849 --> 00:07:36.650
What they wanted was a highly intelligent
machine also known as a computer, and this

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:39.669
is what got IBM into the PC game.

00:07:39.669 --> 00:07:45.259
Unfortunately, Watson never got to see this
evolution as he would pass away in 1956, but

00:07:45.259 --> 00:07:49.650
he very much laid the foundation for IBM’s
pivot into the PC market.

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:54.800
Just one year after Watson passed away, IBM
would introduce the programming language Fortran.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:01.639
IBM would follow this up with a slew of computers
such as the IBM 1401 and the IBM 1403, but

00:08:01.639 --> 00:08:07.300
the breakthrough didn’t happen till 1964
when they introduced the IBM system 360.

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:11.349
The feature that set apart this computer was
the fact that the hardware and software were

00:08:11.349 --> 00:08:12.930
no longer bundled.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:16.379
In other words, you could upgrade and swap
software.

00:08:16.379 --> 00:08:21.259
This proved to be instrumental for NASA and
NASA would end up using IBM computers to land

00:08:21.259 --> 00:08:23.879
the first humans on the moon in 1969.

00:08:23.879 --> 00:08:29.680
This was no doubt the golden age for IBM,
but Watson’s fears from 25 years ago would

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:39.870
start coming to fruition as the government
heavily pulled back spending after winning

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:40.870
the space race.

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:41.959
PAINFUL FAILURE:
While the govenrment pulled back their contracts,

00:08:41.959 --> 00:08:45.390
IBM was by no means in any distress.

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:51.160
Over the past 30 years, IBM had built an international
brand and had major companies as clients.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:54.040
So, they were by no means going to die.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:59.020
But, given that they had maxed out the corporate
and government side of things, IBM was forced

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:02.880
to look for new ways to grow and this led
to the public sector.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:07.890
The only problem was that IBM had virtually
no experience selling to everyday consumers

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:09.970
and it would show.

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:15.380
IBM didn’t know it at the time though and
they would launch project SCAMP in 1973.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:18.970
This project is what would eventually lead
to the introduction of IBM’s first personal

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:22.579
computer in 1981: the IBM 5150.

00:09:22.579 --> 00:09:27.270
This computer actually turned out to be a
massive success because it made PCs relatively

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:29.180
affordable for the first time.

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:32.820
The 5150 launched for a price of $1,600.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:38.070
And while that was by no means cheap as it
translates to $5200 today, it was way cheaper

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:40.029
than everything else on the market.

00:09:40.029 --> 00:09:44.680
The Xerox Star, for example, came in at an
eyewatering $16,500.

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:51.820
Meanwhile, the Apple Macintosh came in at
$2,500, so for budget oriented customers,

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:55.960
which is most customers, the 5150 was the
top choice.

00:09:55.960 --> 00:10:01.040
This would allow for IBM to dominate the market
throughout the early 1980s, but there was

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:03.079
just major problem.

00:10:03.079 --> 00:10:06.640
People weren’t buying IBM computers because
there was some sort of unique feature to them

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:08.950
or because people loved the brand.

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:14.170
Rather, much of IBM’s popularity could be
explained by their price, and this is never

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:19.640
a good thing as it almost always leads in
a race to the bottom, and that’s exactly

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:20.800
what happened.

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:25.399
Dozens of new computer manufacturers like
Dell popped up, but there was one key difference

00:10:25.399 --> 00:10:28.950
between manufacturers like IBM and manufacturers
like Dell.

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:33.510
The difference was that companies like Dell
weren’t computer manufacturers at all really,

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:34.570
they were computer assemblers.

00:10:34.570 --> 00:10:39.540
They didn’t create new architectures or
put together software or any of that.

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:44.220
They just sourced the cheapest parts on the
market, installed MS Dos, and called it a

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:45.220
day.

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:48.420
This allowed them to undercut IBM by significant
margins.

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:53.770
The Dell Turbo for example costed a mere $795
which was half of IBM.

00:10:53.770 --> 00:10:58.670
And given that IBM hadn’t built any brand
loyalty, people switched to these cheaper

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:00.070
computers in a heartbeat.

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:05.000
And the truth was that IBM simply couldn’t
compete at those prices.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:09.070
They simply had far more overhead and R&amp;D
expenses.

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:11.640
IBM didn’t want to call it quits either
though.

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:17.610
So, they just stuck around as their PC marketshare
waned year after year after year.

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:21.720
And, it wasn’t till after the dotcom bubble
that they would finally decide to pull the

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:24.850
plus and sell their PC business to Lenovo.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:29.420
And that marked the end of IBM’s foray into
consumer products.

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:35.100
RESURRECTION:
While IBM had gotten destroyed in the consumer

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:39.600
PC market, their commercial business was still
doing alright.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:44.459
So they decided to double down on commercial
customers, and this led them to a new goldmine

00:11:44.459 --> 00:11:48.800
which was servers, databases, and of course
cloud computing.

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:53.529
Pretty soon, IBM was starting to turn things
around and it seemed like IBM was very much

00:11:53.529 --> 00:11:54.529
resurrected.

00:11:54.529 --> 00:11:58.450
But, it’s not all sunshines and rainbows
for IBM because their freedom within the cloud

00:11:58.450 --> 00:12:01.170
market didn’t last all that long.

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:05.320
Since then, basically every company under
the sun has jumped onto the market whether

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:09.720
it be Apple, Google, Dell, Oracle, or Microsoft.

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:14.390
They all want a piece of the pie and this
has led to IBM shedding much of it’s cloud

00:12:14.390 --> 00:12:15.390
gains.

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:19.149
In fact, IBM is very much in a downtrend once
again.

00:12:19.149 --> 00:12:25.230
In the past 10 years, IBM’s revenue has
nearly halved from over $100 billion to barely

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:26.450
$60 billion.

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:30.540
A similar story can be seen with their net
income as well which has shrunk from over

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:34.639
$15 billion to just $1.2 billion.

00:12:34.639 --> 00:12:38.399
It seems like investors are still giving IBM
a good amount of credit as they’ve been

00:12:38.399 --> 00:12:41.449
able to maintain a pe ratio of over a 100.

00:12:41.449 --> 00:12:46.389
But, if IBM continues to put up lackluster
performance, it’s not clear how much longer

00:12:46.389 --> 00:12:49.949
investors will give them leeway, but that’s
just what I think.

00:12:49.949 --> 00:12:52.360
Do you think IBM has a future?

